---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import ProfilePicture from '../tools/ProfilePicture';
import { getTool } from '../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('profile-picture-maker');
---

<ToolPage tool={tool}>
  <ProfilePicture slot="tool" client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="png-or-jpg">Transparent PNG or JPG with a background?</h2>
      <p>
        A circle cut out of a rectangular image leaves four corners that have to be something.
        Your two options behave quite differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Transparent PNG</strong> leaves those corners empty, so the circle sits correctly
        on any background colour. This is the safer choice: if a platform displays your avatar on
        a dark header one day and a light card the next, a transparent PNG looks right in both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>JPG with a background colour</strong> fills the corners with whatever you pick.
        The file is smaller, and some upload forms still reject PNG. The risk is a visible square
        of the wrong colour behind your circle — so match the colour to where it will actually
        appear, or use PNG.
      </p>
      <p>
        Worth knowing: most platforms crop avatars to a circle themselves, so pre-cropping is
        mainly about controlling the framing rather than the shape. Doing it yourself means you
        decide what gets cut, instead of an automatic centre crop deciding for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="size">What size to export</h2>
      <p>
        Larger than the minimum, always. Platforms downscale whatever you give them, and they do
        it well — but none of them will upscale, so uploading something exactly at the stated
        minimum leaves no headroom when a redesign increases the display size.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Platform</th>
              <th>Recommended upload</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>YouTube</td><td>800 x 800</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Facebook</td><td>512 x 512</td></tr>
            <tr><td>LinkedIn</td><td>400 x 400</td></tr>
            <tr><td>X</td><td>400 x 400</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Instagram</td><td>320 x 320</td></tr>
            <tr><td>TikTok</td><td>200 x 200</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Exporting a single 800 by 800 image covers every one of these. There is no benefit to
        matching each platform's exact number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="framing">Framing a face that reads at 40 pixels</h2>
      <p>
        Avatars are displayed small — often 40 pixels across in a comment thread, sometimes
        smaller. That single fact should drive every framing decision.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Fill most of the circle with your head and shoulders. A full-body shot becomes an
          unidentifiable smudge.
        </li>
        <li>
          Leave a small margin above the head. Cropping right at the hairline looks cramped, and
          platforms sometimes crop slightly tighter than your preview.
        </li>
        <li>
          Favour a plain or blurred background. Detail behind you competes with your face at
          small sizes and wins.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check the contrast between you and the background. At 40 pixels, contrast is what makes
          a face recognisable, not detail.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="blurry">Why avatars come out blurry</h2>
      <p>
        Nearly always because the source was too small. If your original photo is 500 pixels wide
        and your face occupies a third of it, the face is around 170 pixels — so a 400 pixel
        avatar is being enlarged more than twice, and no amount of processing recovers detail
        that was never captured.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool warns you when the area you have framed is smaller than the output size you have
        chosen. If you see that warning, the fix is a larger source photo, not a different
        setting. Failing that, drop the output size to something the source can actually support.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second cause is platform compression. Most services re-encode avatars aggressively.
        You cannot control that, but giving them a clean, sharp, correctly sized image to start
        from produces a noticeably better result than giving them something already soft.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
